{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    # dp[i][j] stores the count of sequences of length i ending with j.\n    # m and n are assumed to be positive integers as per critical requirements.\n    dp = [[0] * (m + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n    # Base case: sequences of length 1. Each element from 1 to m is a valid sequence.\n    for j in range(1, m + 1):\n        dp[1][j] = 1\n\n    # Fill the DP table for lengths from 2 to n.\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        # Calculate prefix sums for dp[i-1] to efficiently sum previous counts.\n        prefix_sum_prev_length = [0] * (m + 1)\n        for k_val in range(1, m + 1):\n            prefix_sum_prev_length[k_val] = prefix_sum_prev_length[k_val - 1] + dp[i - 1][k_val]\n\n        for j in range(1, m + 1):\n            # A previous element 'k' must satisfy 2*k <= j.\n            max_prev_element = j // 2\n            if max_prev_element >= 1:\n                dp[i][j] = prefix_sum_prev_length[max_prev_element]\n\n    # The total number of sequences of length n is the sum of all dp[n][j] for j from 1 to m.\n    total_sequences = sum(dp[n][j] for j in range(1, m + 1))\n    return total_sequences"}
