{"task_id": "Mbpp/273", "solution": "class TupleLengthMismatchError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when two tuples have different lengths for an element-wise operation.\"\"\"\n    pass\nclass TupleElementTypeError(TypeError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when a tuple contains non-numeric elements where numeric elements are expected.\"\"\"\n    pass\ndef substract_elements(tuple1: tuple[int | float, ...], tuple2: tuple[int | float, ...]) -> tuple[int | float, ...]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Subtracts elements of the second tuple from the first tuple at corresponding indices.\n\n    Args:\n        tuple1: The first tuple, containing numeric elements.\n        tuple2: The second tuple, containing numeric elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new tuple containing the results of the subtraction.\n\n    Raises:\n        TupleLengthMismatchError: If input tuples have different lengths.\n        TupleElementTypeError: If any element in the input tuples is not an int or float.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(tuple1) != len(tuple2):\n        raise TupleLengthMismatchError(\"Input tuples must have the same length.\")\n\n    result = []\n    for i in range(len(tuple1)):\n        val1 = tuple1[i]\n        val2 = tuple2[i]\n\n        if not isinstance(val1, (int, float)) or not isinstance(val2, (int, float)):\n            raise TupleElementTypeError(\"All tuple elements must be numeric (int or float).\")\n        \n        result.append(val1 - val2)\n    \n    return tuple(result)"}
