{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(num: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Sets the lowest-indexed (rightmost) unset bit in an integer.\n    For negative one, which has all bits set in two's complement, the number is returned unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    if num == -1:\n        return num\n\n    bit_pos = 0\n    while True:\n        # Check if the bit at the current position `bit_pos` is 0\n        if not (num & (1 << bit_pos)):\n            # If it's 0, set this bit to 1 and return the result\n            return num | (1 << bit_pos)\n        \n        # If the bit is 1, move to the next higher bit position\n        bit_pos += 1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets the leftmost (most significant) unset bit in a non-negative integer.\n    Raises ValueError if the input is negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Find the position of the most significant bit that is currently 0.\n    # Start checking from the MSB down to the LSB.\n    msb_pos = n.bit_length() - 1\n\n    for i in range(msb_pos, -1, -1):\n        if not (n & (1 << i)):  # If the bit at position i is 0\n            return n | (1 << i)  # Set that bit and return the new value.\n\n    # If all bits up to msb_pos are set (e.g., n=7 which is 0111),\n    # then the next bit to the left (msb_pos + 1) is the unset one.\n    return n | (1 << (msb_pos + 1))"}
